WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.820
Parsing is a security boundary.

2
00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:19.160
C# developers often parse JSON, XML, form data, binary formats, and serialized objects as routine plumbing, but parsers can affect object creation, resource access, type selection, validation, and application behavior.

3
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:27.820
Unsafe deserialization is especially important in legacy patterns where serialized data can influence which types are created or which code paths execute.

4
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:34.880
Treat externally supplied serialized data as hostile unless the format and parser are intentionally constrained.

5
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.800
Modern JSON model binding is convenient, but it still deserves review.

6
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:50.540
A model binder may populate fields the developer did not expect, accept deeply nested structures, bind collections with surprising sizes, or create partially valid objects.

7
00:00:50.640 --> 00:01:02.320
Over-posting and mass assignment happen when clients can set properties that should be controlled only by the server, such as roles, ownership, approval state, tenant identifiers, or workflow transitions.

8
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.460
Binding should be paired with request-specific models and server-side authorization.

9
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.860
XML processing has its own risk profile.

10
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:20.800
Parser features, external resource resolution, document type handling, entity expansion, schema validation, and XPath evaluation can all matter.

11
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:38.900
The safe configuration depends on the application, but the habit is consistent: enable only what the application needs, avoid external resource access by default, constrain document size and complexity, and validate shape before XML content influences queries, files, or downstream operations.

12
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:45.180
Safe parser choices reduce injection opportunities because they keep input in a predictable structure.

13
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:58.720
Use well-supported libraries, avoid legacy binary serializers for untrusted data, prefer explicit DTOs over broad object graphs, enforce size and depth limits, and document the parser settings expected by the application.

14
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:06.980
A parser is not just a convenience function; it is where outside bytes become application objects, so it belongs in secure code review and testing.
